---
/**
 * Act II, section 06: one pull request, from opened to torn down.
 *
 * This is the section that answers the CTO's question rather than the developer's, so the notes
 * under the pipeline are about process — where the runner lives, who reviews the pipeline, what
 * happens to the environment nobody remembers to delete — not about features.
 *
 * The pipeline runs full width rather than in a column beside the notes: its six steps lay out
 * horizontally above a 1000 px viewport, and squeezing them into two thirds of the page collides
 * the step titles with each other. The runner numbers are chips on the surface itself, so the notes
 * do not repeat them.
 */
import { CicdPanel } from '../../surfaces/CicdPanel';
import SectionHead from './SectionHead.astro';
---

<section class="b-band b-band--deep" id="s06" data-act="1">
  <div class="b-shell">
    <SectionHead act="1" index="06" kicker="Console · CI/CD">
      <Fragment slot="title">Every pull request gets its own AWS.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="lede">
        Open a PR and the pipeline builds it on a runner inside your own account, then deploys a stage called{' '}
        <code>pr-128</code> with its own database, its own domain and its own everything. Merge it, and the stage
        deletes itself.
      </Fragment>
    </SectionHead>

    <figure class="b-figure b-reveal">
      <div class="b-figure__stage site-panel">
        <CicdPanel />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="b-figure__caption">
        <b>The numbers in that chip row are why this is affordable to leave on.</b> A preview environment that costs a
        build service per pull request gets switched off by the third sprint.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ul class="b-notes b-notes--row b-reveal">
      <li>
        <h3>Reviewed like the rest of the code</h3>
        <p>
          The pipeline lives in the same config file as the infrastructure. A change to how you deploy arrives as a
          diff, in a pull request, next to the change to what you deploy.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>A real environment, not a mock</h3>
        <p>
          <code>pr-128</code> is a Stacktape stage like any other. The same resources, the same{' '}
          <code>connectTo</code> wiring, the same commands — so "it worked in preview" means something.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Cleanup is the default</h3>
        <p>
          Teardown is part of the pipeline, not a Friday task. Orphaned stacks are the reason most teams stop doing
          per-PR environments after a quarter.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Runners in your account</h3>
        <p>
          Builds run on EC2 inside your own AWS account, and the same runners can back self-hosted GitHub Actions
          runners. Your source and your build cache never leave it.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
